L0231: Built-in proof form called like a function

A compiler form such as rewrite or unfold is written with an exclamation mark: rewrite!(equation, evidence). These forms elaborate checked proof terms; omitting ! asks ordinary name resolution for a different callable.

Example from `tests/corpus/reject/bare_proof_form.lc`; the full file is a regression test.

```rust
// `rewrite`, `unfold`, and `fold` written as calls: the forms take `!` now,
// and each is reported with the fix. A `fold` of one's own is a function.
#[terminates] #[no_panic] #[no_io] logic fn nonzero(x: Int) -> Prop { prop!(x != 0) }

#[terminates] #[no_panic] #[no_io] fn use_nonzero(n: u8, h: @nonzero(n)) -> @(n != 0) {
    unfold(nonzero, h)
}

#[terminates] #[no_panic] #[no_io] fn moved(a: u8, b: u8, same: @(a == b), small: @(a <= 9)) -> @(b <= 9) {
    rewrite(same, small)
}

fn fold(n: u8) -> u8 { n }

fn folded(n: u8) -> u8 { fold(n) }

fn prove(n: u8) -> u8 { n }
```

Language, as built: [1.7:1](../../atlas.html#/doc/language/spec-1.7:1).
